# Explanation: cargo-cicd Design

<!-- BEGIN custom:why-this-exists -->
## Why This Exists

`cargo-cicd` exists because Rust workspaces need a local-first CI/CD helper that keeps
builds clean, fast, and push-ready without requiring a remote CI service for every check.

Local feedback loops should be fast and honest. `cargo-cicd` manufactures a `cicd.toml`
artifact that records workspace state so remote CI can trust what was verified locally.
<!-- END custom:why-this-exists -->

<!-- BEGIN custom:tradeoffs -->
## Tradeoffs

**Local-first vs. remote-authoritative**
Running checks locally before push means faster feedback but requires discipline to not
skip checks. `cargo-cicd` makes skipping visible by omitting entries from `cicd.toml`.

**cicd.toml as carrier**
Publishing state to `cicd.toml` in the workspace root makes state inspectable by both
humans and downstream tooling. The tradeoff is that `cicd.toml` must be kept in sync
with actual run history — stale entries are worse than missing ones.

**Noun-verb command structure**
Commands follow a `noun verb` pattern (e.g., `workspace check`) for discoverability and
composability. The tradeoff is slightly more typing compared to single-word commands.
<!-- END custom:tradeoffs -->

<!-- BEGIN custom:architecture-notes -->
## Architecture Notes

`cargo-cicd` is structured as a clap-based CLI with a noun-verb command hierarchy.
Each command pair maps to an operator in the manufacturing pipeline and emits a
process event that can be validated by downstream tooling.
<!-- END custom:architecture-notes -->
